You are working in the JavaScript repository /luxon (the luxon date library, tests via jest). Your job is a single, precise bug fix in /luxon/src/interval.js, then verification. Do not change any other file, and do not modify any existing test file.

THE BUG: `Interval.fromISO(text, opts)` currently calls `DateTime.fromISO(s, opts)` and `DateTime.fromISO(e, opts)` directly. When `Settings.throwOnInvalid === true`, `DateTime.fromISO` THROWS on unparseable input, so a perfectly valid duration-based interval such as `Interval.fromISO("2016-01-01/P1Y")` or `Interval.fromISO("P1Y/2016-01-01")` throws instead of returning the interval. It should instead fall through to the duration-parsing branches and return the valid Interval (and only throw for genuinely unparsable input, via `Interval.invalid(...)`).

THE FIX: replace the body of `static fromISO(text, opts)` in /luxon/src/interval.js (currently lines ~134-157) with exactly this:

```js
  static fromISO(text, opts) {
    const [s, e] = (text || "").split("/", 2);
    if (s && e) {
      let start, startIsValid;
      try {
        start = DateTime.fromISO(s, opts);
        startIsValid = start.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        startIsValid = false;
      }

      let end, endIsValid;
      try {
        end = DateTime.fromISO(e, opts);
        endIsValid = end.isValid;
      } catch (e) {
        endIsValid = false;
      }

      if (startIsValid && endIsValid) {
        return Interval.fromDateTimes(start, end);
      }

      if (startIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(e, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.after(start, dur);
        }
      } else if (endIsValid) {
        const dur = Duration.fromISO(s, opts);
        if (dur.isValid) {
          return Interval.before(end, dur);
        }
      }
    }
    return Interval.invalid("unparsable", `the input "${text}" can't be parsed asISO 8601`);
  }
```

Keep the surrounding JSDoc comment untouched. Keep code style (prettier, 2-space indent, double quotes).

