=== BUG A: padStart does not pad negative numbers correctly ===
Reported: "There is a bug in src/impl/util.js. Once it is fixed, the test `DateTime#toISODate() correctly pads negative years` should pass."
Current code (around line 101):
```js
  export function padStart(input, n = 2) {
    if (input.toString().length < n) {
      return ("0".repeat(n) + input).slice(-n);
    } else {
      return input.toString();
    }
  }
```
This is reached by `DateTime#toISODate()` because `toTechFormat` builds a Formatter with `forceSimple: true`, so `Formatter#num` calls `padStart(n, p)` directly. For a negative year such as -21 with p=4 the current code returns `"0-21"` instead of `"-0021"`, so `DateTime.fromObject({year: -21, month: 5, day: 25, zone: "utc"}).toISODate()` yields `"0-21-05-25"` instead of `"-0021-05-25"`.
FIX: replace the whole function body with exactly:
```js
  export function padStart(input, n = 2) {
    const isNeg = input < 0;
    let padded;
    if (isNeg) {
      padded = "-" + ("" + -input).padStart(n, "0");
    } else {
      padded = ("" + input).padStart(n, "0");
    }
    return padded;
  }
```
(keep the `export function padStart(input, n = 2) {` signature and 2-space indentation; do not change any other function).

=== BUG B: objToLocalTS is wrong for years 0-99 when the month overflows the year ===
Reported: "There is a bug in src/impl/util.js. Once it is fixed, the test `DateTime#plus works across the 100 barrier when passing through February` should pass."
Current code (around line 155):
```js
  export function objToLocalTS(obj) {
    let d = Date.UTC(
      obj.year,
      obj.month - 1,
      obj.day,
      obj.hour,
      obj.minute,
      obj.second,
      obj.millisecond
    );

    // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
    if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
      d = new Date(d);
      d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);
    }
    return +d;
  }
```
Mechanism: `Date.UTC(99, 13, 28)` (i.e. year 99 with an overflowing month, which is what `DateTime#plus` produces when adding months/days across the year boundary) yields February of year 100, so `d.getUTCFullYear()` is 100 and subtracting 1900 gives -1800 instead of 99/100. The month/day must be re-applied together with the year.
FIX: replace the body of the `if` block with exactly:
```js
    // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
    if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
      d = new Date(d);
      // set the month and day again, this is necessary because year 2000 is a leap year, but year 100 is not
      // so if we are at 2000-02-29 and we set the year to 100, we get 0100-03-01
      d.setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day);
    }
    return +d;
```
Change nothing else in the file.

